WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.592 --> 00:00:08.050
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN:
Thanks, everyone.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:09.810
My name is Amy Krishnamohan.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.630
I do product marketing
at Google Cloud.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:16.140
I'm here with really exciting
guest speakers today,

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:19.928
Joe and Seph from Kolide
and the 6 River Systems.

00:00:19.928 --> 00:00:21.720
Today we're going to
talk a little bit more

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.890
about how you can build
a SaaS application

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:27.210
in the cloud-based
infrastructure.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.100
So SaaS has changed
a lot, right?

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.910
When you had more on-prem,
hardware-based environment,

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.150
having a single tenancy was
actually really expensive

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.770
but is very secure.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.210
But cloud
infrastructure actually

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.310
made a really drastic
change in terms

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.560
of making these
instances available

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:44.970
very easily at your hand.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:50.620
So SaaS is obviously making
leading changes in that area.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.790
So here I'll
introduce our guest.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:58.887
Joe, would you like
to take it from here?

00:00:58.887 --> 00:01:00.220
JOE HUGHES: Hey, I'm Joe Hughes.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:03.850
I'm director of devops
at 6 River Systems.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.490
Six River Systems is
a robotic company.

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:10.370
When you get things shipped
to you from the internet,

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:11.950
we have a big hand
in making sure

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:15.730
that those things can get to
you in two days or faster.

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:17.680
And I was previously
at a company called

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:21.850
Amazon Robotics in that
space, and you probably

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.320
know them as Kiva.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:26.940
They solve the same problem.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.280
So that's our robot.

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:30.050
His name is Chuck.

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.360
And essentially
Chuck leads a worker

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.750
around a warehouse using kind
of self-driving technology,

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.620
identifying objects
in the way and taking

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.050
that person to fulfill
your order of toothbrushes

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:45.420
or whatever you happened
to order off the internet.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.310
This is what those
warehouses look like.

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:51.840
So this isn't your Home
Depot or your Target store.

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.180
This is thousands and
thousands of products,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.680
million-square-foot warehouses.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.950
And so we have algorithms
in the cloud that

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:02.310
helps us batch these
orders and get efficiencies

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:05.100
for our customers and
make sure that Chuck

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.740
or the mini Chucks that
are in these warehouses

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:13.850
coordinate together and make
life better for our customers.

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:19.180
So if you're not familiar
with warehouse robotics,

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.040
traditionally what
you would be doing

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.670
is carrying around
a big cart weighing

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.070
upwards of 50 to 100 pounds.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:30.310
Now you can follow
Chuck around and have

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:31.540
a more enjoyable workday.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.480
Basically, this is
just a quick look

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.190
at our high-level architecture.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:45.460
And this is kind of what Amy was
alluding to is we have a Google

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:48.430
Kubernetes Engine cluster--

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.680
Kubernetes is a
container scheduler--

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.580
where we create what
are called namespaces,

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:57.950
which basically isolate
customers from one another.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.970
We actually run these namespaces
on dedicated nodes, which

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.430
is like dedicated
compute, and we actually

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:06.760
have a dedicated SQL
primary and a read replica

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:08.660
for every single customer.

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.110
So that means that
data from one warehouse

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.270
never gets mixed with another.

00:03:14.270 --> 00:03:16.730
So that's a little
bit about what I do

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:17.810
and what my company does.

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:19.250
I'm going to hand
it over to Seph

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:21.042
to kind of fill you in
on what Kolide does.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:25.840
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
Hi, everyone.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:26.340
I'm Seph.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.070
I'm an SRE at Kolide,
which does sort

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:32.400
of infrastructure analytics
and security analytics.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.380
Previously I've been at
Fastly, Twitter, ActBlue.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:38.400
I like infrastructure as
code, data simplification,

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.260
stuff like that.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:44.940
Kolide builds an endpoint that
hangs out on devices, primarily

00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:48.210
laptops, and collects
analytics about that

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.010
and sends it into
our cloud where

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.740
we do some analysis on it.

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:56.580
We launched Cloud, which is
our SaaS offering, about a year

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.630
ago now.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:01.500
And in that, each customer has
an isolated namespace, very

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:03.780
similar to what Joe is doing.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.630
And each namespace has its own
little stack of application,

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:08.730
server, and
data-processing elements,

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:14.250
and those back call to
dedicated Cloud SQL instances.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.459
So kind of did that
because it reduces

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:20.110
the noisy-neighbor problems.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:24.210
It also shifts the
burden of data isolation

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:27.820
from application developers
to infrastructure.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:29.460
We dedicate
infrastructure resources

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.610
to ensure data segregation,
and then we can take it out

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.380
of the application.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.130
We do share our edge ingress.

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.580
So edge is shared, and
it then comes in and gets

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:41.950
sort of pushed into the back.

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:44.320
We use Kubernetes
quite extensively.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.600
We follow the operator pattern.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:50.320
So each customer has a
custom resource definition,

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.430
and then an operator
goes and reads the CRD,

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:57.490
spawns the namespace,
creates many deployments.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:01.150
Each one brings up things,
brings up Cloud SQL.

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:04.660
We started running into issues
around the number of namespaces

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.040
and things that can be in a
single Kubernetes cluster,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.900
so we have multiple
Kubernetes clusters now.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:12.675
We found that some
of the spin-up times

00:05:12.675 --> 00:05:14.050
were a little
slow, so we started

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:18.400
precreating unallocated
customers and then, on sign up,

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.010
would allocate them.

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:24.700
We are very soon launching
a new product next week

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.790
which moves away from the
single-tenant architecture

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:30.460
and starts revisiting
multitenant designs.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:35.490
So that uses foreign keys
for customer data isolation,

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:41.860
and this is enforced
at a data layer

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.530
by making sure there are
effectively SQL WHERE

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:45.970
clauses all over.

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:48.640
So it's basically
our data library,

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:51.220
and we could shard that by
customer to separate back ends.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:55.980
So I think that's all
I've got prepared.

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:56.980
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Great.

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:57.750
Thanks.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.280
So before we get
started, there is

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.158
a Dory open in your mobile app.

00:06:01.158 --> 00:06:03.700
So if you have any questions,
feel free to submit a question.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:06.010
We'll take the
questions at the end.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:09.420
And so there are
many ways that we

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:12.100
can define the multitenant
environment, right?

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:14.140
Everybody defines it
in very different way.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.470
So how would you
define multitenant

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:20.150
in the cloud environment?

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:23.675
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS: I guess
I have the mic, so I'll start.

00:06:23.675 --> 00:06:26.050
I think there is a whole lot
of ways to think about that.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:30.320
We run in Google's cloud,
so inherently Google's cloud

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:31.340
is multitenant.

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:34.370
Probably almost everyone in
this room is in Google's cloud.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:37.750
But we run on our own
Kubernetes cluster,

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:39.950
but our Kubernetes cluster
is also multitenant

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:44.330
because many of our customers
are in the same Kubernetes

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:46.730
cluster, so our Kubernetes
cluster is single tenant.

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:50.085
But each daemon or pod inside--

00:06:50.085 --> 00:06:53.580
and Kubernetes workloads
are grouped by pod.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:56.240
So a pod is like a set
of linked containers.

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:58.070
And each pod is single tenant.

00:06:58.070 --> 00:06:59.990
So only one customer
is in a pod.

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:03.140
And our underlying database
is we use Cloud SQL.

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:06.420
We have one Cloud SQL
instance per customer.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:09.140
So that's also a very
clear single tenant.

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:11.660
So it kind of depends
on where you look at it.

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:13.820
I would generally
describe our cloud product

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:19.460
as being single tenant since
Google Cloud being multitenant

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:22.550
isn't really very interesting,
but our application servers

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:24.110
and our database servers are.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:29.750
JOE HUGHES: Yeah, I don't
really have much to add to that.

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:33.500
I would say that a lot
of it also comes down

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:35.430
to your business problem.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:39.650
And for us, when
we considered what

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:41.270
we should build we
were like, well,

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:43.220
we have long lead times
with our customers,

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:46.640
and they may or may not
want us to upgrade them.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:50.280
And so what is this
architecture called?

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:52.250
And it's called single
tenant because we

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:56.390
have really tight control over
how our customer environment is

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:57.860
managed.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:07:59.925
That works well
for us, but if you

00:07:59.925 --> 00:08:02.240
were a building a
social-media platform

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.070
or something like
that, it's probably

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:05.570
not the right
architecture for you.

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:08.480
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: So you're
defining single tenant

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:12.560
is having a customer
per pod, and then also

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:16.202
each single customer would have
a dedicated database instance.

00:08:16.202 --> 00:08:16.910
JOE HUGHES: Yeah.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:19.420
And we go a step
further than Seph

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:24.830
where we actually have dedicated
Google Cloud Platform nodes

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:28.250
that Kubernetes will only
schedule customer pods

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:31.940
and customer workloads
onto those dedicated nodes.

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:34.520
Where I know Seph,
as he spoke about,

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:37.789
his is more multitenant where
a customer's pod can end up

00:08:37.789 --> 00:08:40.309
on any node, and
they may move around.

00:08:40.309 --> 00:08:43.220
Whereas ours, we try to
protect our customers

00:08:43.220 --> 00:08:46.260
by having dedicated compute.

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:49.910
Kubernetes is so flexible
that it makes it easy for Seph

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:53.205
to do his thing and for me to
do it a little bit different.

00:08:53.205 --> 00:08:55.580
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: So when you
designed your architecture,

00:08:55.580 --> 00:09:00.080
what was your biggest
consideration?

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:02.930
JOE HUGHES: For us, it's really
about customer stability.

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:05.060
I think everyone here
would be really angry

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:06.650
if the socks that
were guaranteed

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:09.660
to come to you in two days
were there two weeks later.

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:12.080
And if we caused that
because our system was

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.347
down for some reason
for some amount time,

00:09:15.347 --> 00:09:17.180
then our customers
aren't going to trust us.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:22.650
So we have to really
guarantee that customer A--

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:25.130
you know, the
noisy-neighbor problem,

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:28.110
we can't even let it enter
the equation for our system.

00:09:30.492 --> 00:09:32.450
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
We had two major drivers

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:36.930
for going single tenant
for our last product.

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:39.710
One of them was to reduce
noisy neighbor a lot.

00:09:39.710 --> 00:09:42.290
So again, one customer
does a lot of stuff,

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:44.790
basically starves the
resources of other people,

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:48.420
so we started
splitting up resources.

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:50.780
The other reason is just
around data isolation.

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:53.630
Again, it makes it very easy
for an application developer

00:09:53.630 --> 00:09:54.930
just not even think about it.

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:57.680
You can be like, oh,
there's no way I can select

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:58.940
data for the wrong customer.

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:02.660
It's just that app server
doesn't have access to that.

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:04.160
So that was a big driver for us.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:05.640
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: I see.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:08.810
So going back more
on the database side,

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:11.690
have you guys ever tried
putting a multitenant

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:15.290
in a single database, like
maybe separating them by the key

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:18.290
or by table, things like that?

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:20.840
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
So we do actually.

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:22.820
So I mentioned we're
launching a new product.

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:24.830
Our new product has
a shared database.

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:27.470
So each customer has their
foreign-key constraints.

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:30.380
Every piece of data is
linked to a customer ID,

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:33.080
and there are WHERE
classes all over.

00:10:33.080 --> 00:10:35.510
But again, it's just
this tradeoff of,

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:37.400
are you doing the
data isolation work

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.230
in your application
at the SQL level

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:41.570
or at an infrastructure level?

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:42.890
So you can go both ways.

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:48.800
JOE HUGHES: So our
company, another factor

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:54.800
of why we haven't looked into
that situation was just time.

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:57.550
We knew that there
would be tradeoffs,

00:10:57.550 --> 00:10:59.840
and that it's more
developer time

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:01.470
to do those sort of things.

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.890
And so where we can
shift complexity away

00:11:03.890 --> 00:11:06.740
from our developers and
shift it onto Google, which

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:10.100
is what we were
really doing, that

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:13.498
made it an easy call for us to
just continue with that model.

00:11:13.498 --> 00:11:15.790
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
Actually, as I think about this,

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:20.020
one of the ongoing tradeoffs for
us around number of instances

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:22.810
is just straight up
number of instances.

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:25.180
When we had 10
instances, it was easy.

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:29.260
But as the number of tenants we
have increases and increases,

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:30.780
we're suddenly now managing--

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:34.000
or Google is managing many,
many, many Cloud SQL instances.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:39.460
And it mostly works great,
but as a single person

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:41.020
managing them, it
gets much harder

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:43.870
for me to track down problems.

00:11:43.870 --> 00:11:46.910
So the number of things
to pay attention to--

00:11:46.910 --> 00:11:48.290
I don't know.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:49.900
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN:
That's interesting.

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:53.050
So now that we
talk about instance

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:56.500
so I'm sure when
your customers create

00:11:56.500 --> 00:11:58.150
their account in
the application,

00:11:58.150 --> 00:12:01.510
I'm sure they want kind of
instant creation, right?

00:12:01.510 --> 00:12:04.900
But creating the database
instance or node or the pod,

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:05.600
it takes time.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:08.593
So how do you manage
that latency issue?

00:12:08.593 --> 00:12:10.010
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
I'll start.

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:13.120
So we have-- sign up
is available online.

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:15.920
So people can just kind of
go, hey, I want to sign up.

00:12:15.920 --> 00:12:18.415
And we used to have a slightly
asynchronous sign-up flow.

00:12:18.415 --> 00:12:19.540
People would click Sign Up.

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:21.998
We'd send them an email, and
then they'd click their email.

00:12:21.998 --> 00:12:24.490
And behind the scenes while
that email was being delivered,

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:26.530
we would spin up a database.

00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:28.060
We'd spin up a tenant.

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:30.410
And we had a couple
of issues with that.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:33.580
One of them is that we wanted
to close that loop even tighter.

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:35.890
We didn't want people to
have to wait for their email.

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:37.570
And the other one
is occasionally

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:39.880
instance creation would
fail, and we never

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:42.980
really built failure handling.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:45.850
So we solved that by
preallocating things.

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:49.450
So we had a little daemon that
runs in the background that

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:53.260
says make sure there are roughly
30 free tenants at any given

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:54.020
time.

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:56.290
So we just sort
of have headroom.

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:58.010
And then when someone
signs up it says,

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:00.190
hey, give me the next
free one, gets a free.

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:03.430
Then it basically says, this
now belongs to this company,

00:13:03.430 --> 00:13:04.970
and then it's used.

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:07.540
And then a little while later
the daemon goes, oh hey, look,

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:09.010
I need to create a new free one.

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:11.200
So we've sort of
tackled that loop

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:12.880
by having a precreated model.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:15.070
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: How do
you come up with that 30

00:13:15.070 --> 00:13:16.270
unallocated instances?

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:17.250
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
Just kind of made it up.

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:18.875
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN:
It's a random number.

00:13:18.875 --> 00:13:20.750
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
I looked at what do we

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:22.180
get from a day of sign ups?

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:23.260
What's our peak?

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:27.160
How much headroom do we
want to have lying around?

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:28.600
I picked a number.

00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:30.988
We used to adjust it more
often, but now it's just--

00:13:30.988 --> 00:13:33.280
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: No AI and
machine learning predicted?

00:13:33.280 --> 00:13:36.547
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
No, not on that one.

00:13:36.547 --> 00:13:38.380
JOE HUGHES: For us,
it's a little bit easier

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:41.800
because you can't just go
online and sign up for Chuck.

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:43.240
You have to engage
with our team,

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:45.850
and there's a long
integration period

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:48.100
where you have to integrate
with customers' systems.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:51.730
So a 10-minute creation
time is lightning fast

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:55.210
for our industry, and we're
really happy with that.

00:13:57.917 --> 00:13:59.500
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN:
So it seems like you

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:01.900
both guys are using the
Kubernetes right now.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:07.670
So how do use Kubernetes
in your SaaS application?

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:11.780
JOE HUGHES: You saw a little
bit of that in my slide.

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:14.270
I think Kubernetes is
really great because

00:14:14.270 --> 00:14:16.520
of its flexibility where
you can isolate things

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:18.517
by namespace, Istio.

00:14:18.517 --> 00:14:20.600
We have a lot of customers
that are very concerned

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:21.225
about security.

00:14:21.225 --> 00:14:24.050
There's potentially
personal information

00:14:24.050 --> 00:14:25.760
in some of the things
that we handle.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:32.270
And so that isolation with
namespaces and encryption

00:14:32.270 --> 00:14:35.470
by default, which the Istio
service mesh kind of gives you,

00:14:35.470 --> 00:14:37.640
are all really
powerful tools for us

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:41.930
that we just get by
using the platform.

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:44.610
So we really reach for
all of those things

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:46.910
to make it easier to manage.

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:48.950
As Seph kind of
alluded to, we've

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:50.640
grown our customer
base quite a bit,

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:54.740
and so there's a nontrivial
amount of SQL instances

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:56.660
to manage, and there's
a nontrivial amount

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:57.890
of namespaces and pods.

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:01.730
But that's where Stackdriver
and a lot of the other products

00:15:01.730 --> 00:15:05.030
that Google offers really
come in to help you.

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:09.057
But yeah, I would say that
the continuous release

00:15:09.057 --> 00:15:10.640
of new features in
Kubernetes has made

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:12.800
it a really great thing for us.

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:16.760
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
Yeah, we love Kubernetes.

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:19.130
It's great.

00:15:19.130 --> 00:15:23.120
I sort of think of it as it's
a nice abstraction for defining

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:23.750
workloads.

00:15:23.750 --> 00:15:26.540
So we define workloads, and
then Kubernetes just kind of

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:27.680
makes them happen.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:32.250
And we heavily use namespaces
as our isolation model.

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:35.420
We also have really done a
lot in the Kubernetes operator

00:15:35.420 --> 00:15:40.220
model, which has a thing,
CoreOS, declared a while ago.

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:44.120
And that means we
create custom resources.

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:49.550
Resources are like an internal
Kubernetes thing, pile of YAML.

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:53.240
And the resource basically
says this is a customer.

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:54.770
They should get
this many resources.

00:15:54.770 --> 00:15:58.250
They're big, medium,
small, and make them go.

00:15:58.250 --> 00:15:59.870
And then we have
an operator that

00:15:59.870 --> 00:16:01.640
comes along, reads
all the resources,

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:05.090
and creates the namespace,
creates all the pods,

00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:07.390
creates the Cloud SQL instance.

00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:10.500
And so yeah,
Kubernetes is great,

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:13.010
and I really like
the operator pattern.

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:15.680
It lets me take my knowledge
of how to create and manage

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:17.780
these things and
translate it into code

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:21.110
that just runs and synchronizes
itself and kind of just

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:24.277
goes in the background and
fixes, fixes, fixes, fixes.

00:16:24.277 --> 00:16:26.110
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: And
the other tools you're

00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:29.930
using together with
Kubernetes, Terraform?

00:16:29.930 --> 00:16:32.530
JOE HUGHES: Yeah, so as you
might have guessed from Amy,

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:33.770
we use Terraform.

00:16:33.770 --> 00:16:37.120
We're actually on the
journey to where Seph already

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:41.080
is where the operator pattern,
we've watched it closely

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:44.230
and kind of seen people
apply it successfully.

00:16:44.230 --> 00:16:45.682
Again, we're in
an industry where

00:16:45.682 --> 00:16:47.390
we have to be a little
bit more cautious.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:50.020
So we have to kind of
watch things and make sure

00:16:50.020 --> 00:16:52.570
that they mature in the way
that we really think that we can

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:55.030
make a long-term bet on them.

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:56.300
Kubernetes is a no-brainer.

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:59.950
The operating pattern, it
is a pattern, and so for us

00:16:59.950 --> 00:17:01.990
we had to see people
successfully apply it.

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:05.619
And there's a lot of great
examples out there now

00:17:05.619 --> 00:17:07.710
and a lot of great stories
from people like Seph

00:17:07.710 --> 00:17:09.579
who have applied
it, so that's where

00:17:09.579 --> 00:17:11.500
we're going to be going next.

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:15.670
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: So Joe, you
touched about the securities.

00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:19.319
In the multitenant environment,
how do you isolate tenant

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:21.446
to avoid any security concern?

00:17:21.446 --> 00:17:24.550
Is there any specific
thing that you can share?

00:17:24.550 --> 00:17:27.010
JOE HUGHES: Yeah, I
think just by the nature

00:17:27.010 --> 00:17:30.800
of having the single tenancy
you alleviate almost all

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:32.500
of that concern.

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:34.780
There are certain
customers where

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:36.580
they want that
data to be on-prem,

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:40.540
and that's a whole another
thing that you have to address.

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:42.700
But in a multitenant
application,

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:46.120
again, I think you really have
to look at your business case.

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:48.700
I've worked for companies that
were under HIPAA compliance.

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:52.590
And so in a
multitenant application

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:54.470
that has to have
HIPAA compliance,

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:56.140
you have to have
row-level encryption.

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:57.848
And then you have to
reach for some tools

00:17:57.848 --> 00:18:00.550
like Vault, which is a
HashiCorp product that

00:18:00.550 --> 00:18:04.540
allows you to have encryption
as a service or other things

00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:05.140
like that.

00:18:05.140 --> 00:18:08.560
Because it's not just
enough that you can--

00:18:08.560 --> 00:18:10.780
if someone was to access
it, they can't even

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:11.770
be able to decrypt it.

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:15.160
And so business case
is what you really

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:18.950
need to think about in a
lot of these situations.

00:18:18.950 --> 00:18:20.450
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
Yeah, I'm not

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:22.533
sure I have much to add
on the single-tenant side.

00:18:22.533 --> 00:18:26.450
It is a great answer for
very strong data isolation.

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:28.500
That said, if you
go multitenant,

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:30.000
you're basically
pushing that burden

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.980
to the application layer.

00:18:32.980 --> 00:18:36.990
In our multitenant world, we
do it via the database adaptor

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:40.500
just enforces that, oh,
this is a call for data?

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:42.780
It had better have
an appropriate WHERE

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:44.910
tenet equals x clause on it.

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:47.063
And frequently to
handle the needs

00:18:47.063 --> 00:18:48.480
of really sensitive
customers, you

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:50.040
end up sharding by customer.

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:52.050
So you end up in a
bit of a hybrid where

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:55.838
most things are multitenant,
but this one is special.

00:18:55.838 --> 00:18:58.130
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: How about
the noisy-neighbor problem?

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:00.070
How do you do the--
perform the isolation?

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:01.820
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
Well, single tenant

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:03.800
makes that pretty easy.

00:19:03.800 --> 00:19:07.260
In Kubernetes, you still have
to think about it a little bit.

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:10.710
So our pods are scheduled
onto the same nodes.

00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:13.430
So we have to make sure
that our pod resource

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:15.020
allocations are correct.

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:19.340
So each of our pods is allocated
so much CPU and so much RAM,

00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:21.750
and then Kubernetes
will just enforce that.

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:25.828
And that's how we approach
the noisy-neighbor problem.

00:19:25.828 --> 00:19:27.870
JOE HUGHES: Yeah, I kind
of gave it away earlier,

00:19:27.870 --> 00:19:32.670
but we actually had a--

00:19:32.670 --> 00:19:37.260
we were doing what Seph
did, and a bug in our code

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:40.950
essentially was causing
cascading failures where

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:43.230
we were bringing
down an entire node,

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:45.600
and there was multiple
customers on that node.

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:49.650
And so our tech-support team
had a lot of angry phone

00:19:49.650 --> 00:19:51.910
calls all at the same time.

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:54.090
So I ended up pulling over
to the side of the road

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:55.590
and doing a lot of
work on my laptop

00:19:55.590 --> 00:19:58.020
on the side of the highway.

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:02.100
So after that we were
like, OK, in the future

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:04.920
when we have more resources
and our company is more mature

00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:08.490
and we can really get a lot
of great people like Seph

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:11.040
and other people like
that, we can attack this.

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:14.340
But for now it's easier,
again, to push that complexity

00:20:14.340 --> 00:20:16.770
to Google, say
we're going to have

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:18.542
node pools for every
single customer,

00:20:18.542 --> 00:20:21.000
and we're going to set up the
Kubernetes rules to make sure

00:20:21.000 --> 00:20:23.850
that no two customers can
end up on the same node

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:27.180
so if something does happen
and we roll out a bug like that

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:30.180
again, it's going to
be completely isolated.

00:20:30.180 --> 00:20:34.050
That is not a long-term
solution, probably,

00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:36.540
but for now it's something
that we can live with,

00:20:36.540 --> 00:20:40.033
and it's helped us continue
to build our business.

00:20:40.033 --> 00:20:42.200
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS: I've
actually sort of got a--

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:44.160
we run into a certain
problem around this

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:46.660
which maybe is funny if people
want to hear about Kubernetes

00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:49.060
problems.

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:50.400
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: No.

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:53.150
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
No, it's great.

00:20:53.150 --> 00:20:59.170
What we've seen is Kubernetes
itself can be a little weird.

00:20:59.170 --> 00:21:02.780
It's not quite a noisy neighbor,
but it's almost similar.

00:21:02.780 --> 00:21:06.200
The underlying Kubernetes
resource requirements per node

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:07.740
are not small.

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:10.150
So they're actually substantial.

00:21:10.150 --> 00:21:12.360
Each node has a
given load, so you

00:21:12.360 --> 00:21:14.670
have to be cognizant of that.

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:17.360
And the other thing we've run
into-- which, I don't know,

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:20.090
I giggle about it, but it's
been a bit of a pain point--

00:21:20.090 --> 00:21:24.472
is some operations we do, if
we go to like upgrade every one

00:21:24.472 --> 00:21:26.180
right, we're basically
running a redeploy

00:21:26.180 --> 00:21:27.762
on every one of our tenants.

00:21:27.762 --> 00:21:29.720
And what we found is that
the Kubernetes master

00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:32.180
can get overwhelmed with
that very easily, and then

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:33.590
everything breaks.

00:21:33.590 --> 00:21:35.890
So it's very bad when your
Kubernetes master breaks.

00:21:35.890 --> 00:21:39.410
So to sort of approach
that scaling and kind

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:42.680
of noisy neighbor, we basically
just rate limit what we do.

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:47.180
So we found that we have to
be very deliberate in how

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:50.120
we roll out upgrades because
otherwise the Kubernetes

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:52.830
master just tries to
do everything at once,

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:54.422
and then it melts.

00:21:54.422 --> 00:21:58.580
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: So how many
pods do you put per node?

00:21:58.580 --> 00:22:02.260
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS: This is
another problem we've run into.

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:04.060
Running in Google,
Kubernetes has

00:22:04.060 --> 00:22:08.830
a limit of 110 pods per
node, which most people are

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:11.560
OK with because you
normally run about 30,

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:13.720
and this is kind
of a pod resource

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:16.570
to node capacity question.

00:22:16.570 --> 00:22:18.370
A lot of our services
are very small,

00:22:18.370 --> 00:22:22.420
and we have a large number of
tenants, so we pack them a bit.

00:22:22.420 --> 00:22:26.950
But what I've found is
when we exceed about 50,

00:22:26.950 --> 00:22:29.620
things start going wonky.

00:22:29.620 --> 00:22:32.140
It's just there's a lot of
auto scaling in the Kubernetes

00:22:32.140 --> 00:22:35.980
system, and a lot of the scaling
around Kubernetes metrics

00:22:35.980 --> 00:22:38.590
collection and logs collection
is based on the idea

00:22:38.590 --> 00:22:40.990
that you will only have
about 30 pods per node.

00:22:40.990 --> 00:22:44.160
So as you start pushing
that higher and higher,

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:46.990
you run into these points
where things you didn't expect

00:22:46.990 --> 00:22:48.070
could break would break.

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:49.070
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: I see.

00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:50.500
So you recommend about 30?

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:51.625
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS: Yes.

00:22:51.625 --> 00:22:54.130
I would recommend not exceeding
30 to 50 pods per node.

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:56.500
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: So do you
have any scaling experience?

00:22:56.500 --> 00:23:00.950
Like maybe in Black Friday, I'm
sure you guys are very busy.

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:05.560
JOE HUGHES: Yeah, so as all of
you would probably recognize,

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:09.670
our customers can have
something like a 20x peak

00:23:09.670 --> 00:23:13.090
where everyone wants to order
something on Black Friday,

00:23:13.090 --> 00:23:14.420
and they want it the next day.

00:23:14.420 --> 00:23:18.430
And so for us, that's
where Kubernetes really

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:21.700
shines for us also is,
OK, we all of a sudden got

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:23.740
20 times the orders
that our system normally

00:23:23.740 --> 00:23:24.940
needs to ingest.

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:28.750
And so being able to both scale
vertically and horizontally

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:33.040
inside Kubernetes gives us
the flexibility to kind of not

00:23:33.040 --> 00:23:35.350
have to worry
about that as much.

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:37.060
We obviously take a
lot of precautions

00:23:37.060 --> 00:23:40.060
and we try to be ready for
that, but as a technology,

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:43.492
it's really enabled us to,
again, do more with less.

00:23:43.492 --> 00:23:45.700
And that's really what I
think a startup is all about

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:49.630
is you need to keep your
most talented people focused

00:23:49.630 --> 00:23:54.610
on the product and not focused
on, oh no, we need more nodes.

00:23:54.610 --> 00:23:56.560
Click the button more.

00:23:56.560 --> 00:24:00.575
And that's where
Kubernetes comes in.

00:24:00.575 --> 00:24:01.950
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
Maybe sort

00:24:01.950 --> 00:24:03.700
of thinking a bit about
that-- though this

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:04.860
might be a future question.

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:05.250
I don't know.

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:05.750
Sorry, Amy.

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:06.833
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: It's OK.

00:24:06.833 --> 00:24:08.500
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
One of the things

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:10.110
that helps us a lot
in handling sort

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:13.520
of momentary bursts in traffic
is we have a predominantly

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:15.240
asynchronous pipeline.

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:17.550
We're trying to reason
about things on a minute

00:24:17.550 --> 00:24:19.480
to couple-of-minute timeline.

00:24:19.480 --> 00:24:21.840
So if a big burst
comes in, it's kind

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:25.450
of just going to hang out in a
queue until we can process it.

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:27.330
So as long as our
queues have capacity,

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:30.150
it's OK if our processing
is a little bit lagging.

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:34.707
And I can't speak for Joe, but
I bet there's something similar.

00:24:34.707 --> 00:24:36.040
JOE HUGHES: We're getting there.

00:24:39.580 --> 00:24:42.060
You think about logistics
and these companies,

00:24:42.060 --> 00:24:45.130
they're often very old.

00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:47.470
And so we're integrating
with systems that expect

00:24:47.470 --> 00:24:48.860
transactions, for example.

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:52.697
So we don't care that we gave
you 2 million things to do.

00:24:52.697 --> 00:24:55.030
We're just going to sit here
and wait for you to tell us

00:24:55.030 --> 00:24:57.050
if you did 2 million things.

00:24:57.050 --> 00:25:00.310
So a lot of our
infrastructure needs to just

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:02.800
really be able to absorb that.

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:05.470
We do some batching
and everything else,

00:25:05.470 --> 00:25:08.320
but it's a problem that
is difficult to solve.

00:25:08.320 --> 00:25:11.210
And the infrastructure can't
always solve that problem,

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:13.870
but good design where you build
queues and everything else

00:25:13.870 --> 00:25:14.510
can help.

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:19.710
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Now let's
move on to more customizability.

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:21.790
So you guys have a
single-tenant environment,

00:25:21.790 --> 00:25:24.795
so how similar to each
tenant the characteristic,

00:25:24.795 --> 00:25:28.380
and if the one tenant needs a
very different characteristic,

00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:30.310
how do you customize
those things?

00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:32.310
JOE HUGHES: Yeah, that's
a really good question.

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:36.330
For us, it's kind of around
the size of our system.

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:41.160
So customer A has 8 Chucks
and customer B has 50.

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:46.930
We put those numbers in,
and out the other side pops,

00:25:46.930 --> 00:25:49.050
oh, well they should get
this size of database,

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:53.280
or they should get
this size of node pool

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:58.020
or this amount of resources
because we exactly how

00:25:58.020 --> 00:25:59.520
a Chuck's going to behave.

00:25:59.520 --> 00:26:01.145
It's a little bit
more difficult if you

00:26:01.145 --> 00:26:04.440
have users or other things
that you can't really predict.

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:07.070
But luckily, we kind of control
both sides of the equation

00:26:07.070 --> 00:26:07.570
there.

00:26:07.570 --> 00:26:11.220
So it can be really
prescriptive for us.

00:26:13.463 --> 00:26:14.880
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
We probably

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:17.660
have about maybe five
variables per customer.

00:26:17.660 --> 00:26:20.520
I don't remember offhand,
but it's about five.

00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:25.140
And we implement that by
environmental variables.

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:27.242
So our application
says, hey, what's

00:26:27.242 --> 00:26:28.950
the value of this
environmental variable,

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:31.000
and it behaves a
little differently.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:34.800
And then we just control those
in our Kubernetes operator

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:35.730
pattern.

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:38.730
Our custom resource
definition has some settings.

00:26:38.730 --> 00:26:42.000
The operator pushes those
in as deployment variables,

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:44.922
and then it all just kind
of follows from there.

00:26:44.922 --> 00:26:46.630
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: That's great.

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:49.380
So now let's move on to
cross-tenant environment.

00:26:49.380 --> 00:26:51.890
So when you need
to do analytics in

00:26:51.890 --> 00:26:54.600
the cross-tenant environment,
how do you do that?

00:26:54.600 --> 00:26:57.452
Are you using any data
warehousing environment?

00:26:57.452 --> 00:26:59.410
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
We don't have that need,

00:26:59.410 --> 00:27:01.890
so we haven't done it.

00:27:01.890 --> 00:27:04.330
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: None.

00:27:04.330 --> 00:27:06.400
JOE HUGHES: We do,
as you would imagine,

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:09.380
really want to know about
all of our customers,

00:27:09.380 --> 00:27:11.330
and so it is an
interesting problem for us.

00:27:11.330 --> 00:27:13.900
And we rely on BigQuery a lot.

00:27:13.900 --> 00:27:16.090
And so all of our robots
are sending information

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:17.710
through Cloud IoT.

00:27:17.710 --> 00:27:20.050
That gets pumped into BigQuery.

00:27:20.050 --> 00:27:22.480
And then we also have
some data pipelines

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:26.802
that go and go to all of our
SQL instances and scrape data,

00:27:26.802 --> 00:27:27.760
shove it into BigQuery.

00:27:27.760 --> 00:27:32.210
So we have one place where
we can look at all of that.

00:27:32.210 --> 00:27:35.450
And we're lucky enough to be
involved with kind of an alpha

00:27:35.450 --> 00:27:41.420
where Cloud SQL is able to be
queried by BigQuery directly.

00:27:41.420 --> 00:27:44.000
And so we'll be happy
to kind of get rid

00:27:44.000 --> 00:27:46.790
of that scraping situation.

00:27:46.790 --> 00:27:48.680
But it's really important.

00:27:48.680 --> 00:27:52.130
That is something
that is a good thing

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:55.010
to reason about is if you're
going to have that need.

00:27:55.010 --> 00:27:56.480
Seph doesn't, but
we definitely do

00:27:56.480 --> 00:27:59.480
because we need to
find efficiencies

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:01.960
across all of our
tenants, and so that's

00:28:01.960 --> 00:28:02.960
really important for us.

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:05.280
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: I see.

00:28:05.280 --> 00:28:08.540
And how do you forecast your
demand, like capacity planning?

00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:11.558
I know you talked
about the 30 instances,

00:28:11.558 --> 00:28:13.100
that you keep it in
your back pocket.

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:15.267
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS: I
mean, our capacity planning

00:28:15.267 --> 00:28:16.230
is relatively simple.

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:18.470
We sort of look at what
our marketing is like

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:20.750
and are we going to a
conference, do we expect

00:28:20.750 --> 00:28:22.690
sign ups, that kind of thing.

00:28:22.690 --> 00:28:25.760
And then we just kind of say,
eh, about this, and we do it.

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:29.370
And overestimate
it a little bit.

00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:30.860
Don't think too hard.

00:28:30.860 --> 00:28:32.420
We're still a very
small company.

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:34.910
We don't have this.

00:28:34.910 --> 00:28:38.320
I guess the other sort of
implicit piece of that is

00:28:38.320 --> 00:28:40.250
we don't have to buy hardware.

00:28:40.250 --> 00:28:45.440
We're just saying, hey, Google,
instead of 30, give me 32.

00:28:45.440 --> 00:28:46.850
It's no different to me.

00:28:46.850 --> 00:28:50.550
It's a minute either way.

00:28:50.550 --> 00:28:52.380
JOE HUGHES: Yeah,
I think we also

00:28:52.380 --> 00:28:55.890
have a pretty nice luxury
where we kind of know.

00:28:55.890 --> 00:28:57.990
Our customers even
tell us how many orders

00:28:57.990 --> 00:29:00.000
they expect on
their Black Friday.

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:03.570
And so we prepare for that by
doing a lot of load testing,

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:05.220
and that really
helps us understand,

00:29:05.220 --> 00:29:08.770
uh oh, this needs to
auto scale this way,

00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:11.880
or we need to prepare
our environment this way.

00:29:11.880 --> 00:29:14.370
So a little easier
for us because we're

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:16.670
dealing with large
enterprise businesses.

00:29:16.670 --> 00:29:18.795
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: Do you
guys do any stress testing

00:29:18.795 --> 00:29:20.365
in your environment?

00:29:20.365 --> 00:29:22.740
JOE HUGHES: Yeah, actually
one of the cool things that we

00:29:22.740 --> 00:29:26.340
do in Kubernetes-- as
you probably can imagine,

00:29:26.340 --> 00:29:29.460
if we need to test
a hundred robots,

00:29:29.460 --> 00:29:32.040
we can't really just go with a
hundred robots to a warehouse

00:29:32.040 --> 00:29:33.360
and deploy them.

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:35.070
That's kind of a lot of money.

00:29:35.070 --> 00:29:37.655
So we actually
run our simulator.

00:29:37.655 --> 00:29:39.030
So we run the same
code that runs

00:29:39.030 --> 00:29:40.860
in the robots in Kubernetes.

00:29:40.860 --> 00:29:42.900
We scale it up to
silly things that

00:29:42.900 --> 00:29:45.060
wouldn't be practical
in the real world,

00:29:45.060 --> 00:29:47.790
and we are then
testing our system

00:29:47.790 --> 00:29:50.670
the same way that it
will be actually stress

00:29:50.670 --> 00:29:51.760
tested in the real world.

00:29:51.760 --> 00:29:53.550
So it's another place
where Kubernetes

00:29:53.550 --> 00:29:56.250
is super useful for
us and another reason

00:29:56.250 --> 00:30:00.262
why it's a really great
platform in general.

00:30:00.262 --> 00:30:02.720
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS: Yeah,
we do something pretty similar

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:03.380
actually.

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:08.180
I mean, no robots for us, but
we run an endpoint on agents,

00:30:08.180 --> 00:30:10.580
and I can just build the
endpoint into containers

00:30:10.580 --> 00:30:12.860
and spin a hundred,
a thousand of them up

00:30:12.860 --> 00:30:15.450
in a Kubernetes cluster and
just point them somewhere.

00:30:15.450 --> 00:30:17.090
So we basically
do the same thing.

00:30:17.090 --> 00:30:19.400
And it's a really easy
way to just sort of

00:30:19.400 --> 00:30:21.010
have a really elastic--

00:30:21.010 --> 00:30:22.190
oh, you know, cool.

00:30:22.190 --> 00:30:23.840
Let's point to
hundreds of these then.

00:30:23.840 --> 00:30:26.530
Well, do we have
enough capacity there,

00:30:26.530 --> 00:30:29.510
which gives us a good
understanding of the number

00:30:29.510 --> 00:30:32.960
of endpoints that can
correspond with what size pods

00:30:32.960 --> 00:30:34.310
and how many pods.

00:30:34.310 --> 00:30:36.988
What does our horizontal
scaling look like?

00:30:36.988 --> 00:30:38.780
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: So
now actually move back

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:40.160
to database question.

00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:41.990
So database is stable.

00:30:41.990 --> 00:30:44.220
So how do you use
together Kubernetes?

00:30:44.220 --> 00:30:48.733
Are you putting database
in the Kubernetes pod?

00:30:48.733 --> 00:30:50.900
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS: So
we don't run our databases

00:30:50.900 --> 00:30:51.920
inside Kubernetes.

00:30:51.920 --> 00:30:53.480
We use Cloud SQL.

00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:54.020
It's great.

00:30:54.020 --> 00:30:54.770
It just runs.

00:30:54.770 --> 00:30:57.320
We don't think
very much about it.

00:30:57.320 --> 00:31:00.320
The way Kubernetes
talks to Cloud SQL,

00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:01.970
it's got a couple
of intermediaries.

00:31:01.970 --> 00:31:05.270
So we use the Cloud
SQL Proxy which

00:31:05.270 --> 00:31:07.880
is designed to
proxy between things

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:10.430
like Kubernetes and Cloud SQL.

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:11.360
Google distributes it.

00:31:11.360 --> 00:31:13.340
You just kind of tell it to go.

00:31:13.340 --> 00:31:16.340
And we run Postgres,
which means we also

00:31:16.340 --> 00:31:18.380
need some additional
connection pooling.

00:31:18.380 --> 00:31:20.870
So each of our customer pods--

00:31:20.870 --> 00:31:23.030
excuse me, each of our
customer namespaces

00:31:23.030 --> 00:31:27.350
has Cloud SQL proxy instances
and PG balancer instances

00:31:27.350 --> 00:31:32.140
as like an additional
connection pooling layer there.

00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:34.840
JOE HUGHES: We actually started
running databases inside

00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:39.490
of containers, again
being more cautious.

00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:43.040
Postgres for Cloud SQL's in
beta, and we were like, well,

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:44.300
we'll just wait.

00:31:44.300 --> 00:31:46.030
And so we waited.

00:31:46.030 --> 00:31:48.940
In that time, we
had decent success.

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:52.150
Kubernetes is a pretty
stable platform.

00:31:52.150 --> 00:31:53.860
Using persistent
volume claims, which

00:31:53.860 --> 00:31:57.340
allows you to have a disk
that follows your pod around,

00:31:57.340 --> 00:31:59.650
we had minimal problems.

00:31:59.650 --> 00:32:03.220
But certainly the
effort was not zero.

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:06.870
I spent a lot of
time tuning that.

00:32:06.870 --> 00:32:09.340
When we had the need
for a read replica,

00:32:09.340 --> 00:32:14.300
that was a couple weeks out of
my time to make that a thing.

00:32:14.300 --> 00:32:17.900
And since moving away
from that model--

00:32:17.900 --> 00:32:19.520
and then there was
also the problems.

00:32:19.520 --> 00:32:23.290
And so the problems are like,
uh oh, alert just went off.

00:32:23.290 --> 00:32:24.730
I'm running out of disk space.

00:32:24.730 --> 00:32:27.550
Got to go in there and
get more disk space.

00:32:27.550 --> 00:32:29.050
That's not a problem
on Cloud SQL.

00:32:29.050 --> 00:32:31.970
Just that auto scales
right up for you.

00:32:31.970 --> 00:32:34.352
And so that was
actually the main thing

00:32:34.352 --> 00:32:35.560
that I was running away from.

00:32:35.560 --> 00:32:38.090
When that finally came out of
beta, I was like OK, great.

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:42.580
No more disk-space problems,
better alerting, and looking

00:32:42.580 --> 00:32:45.640
forward to the day where also
auto scaling on Cloud SQL

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:48.235
takes that out of the
equation for me as well.

00:32:48.235 --> 00:32:49.610
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN:
So whenever you

00:32:49.610 --> 00:32:51.570
have to create an
instance in the Cloud SQL,

00:32:51.570 --> 00:32:55.360
are you using Kubernetes
or Terraform to automate

00:32:55.360 --> 00:32:57.380
that whole process?

00:32:57.380 --> 00:33:02.250
JOE HUGHES: Yeah, so our whole
thing is driven by Terraform.

00:33:02.250 --> 00:33:04.250
I've been using Terraform
for quite a few years.

00:33:04.250 --> 00:33:05.680
It's a great product.

00:33:05.680 --> 00:33:08.170
And what it allows
me to do is create

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:09.820
what's called a
Terraform module, which

00:33:09.820 --> 00:33:12.130
is much like what Seph was
alluding to for an operator,

00:33:12.130 --> 00:33:14.720
but that module represents
what is a customer,

00:33:14.720 --> 00:33:17.290
and that customer is a
Cloud SQL instance, a DNS

00:33:17.290 --> 00:33:20.300
entry, a namespace, and
all this other stuff.

00:33:20.300 --> 00:33:22.753
And then there's this
tool called Atlantis

00:33:22.753 --> 00:33:24.670
which allows you to
manage all of these things

00:33:24.670 --> 00:33:26.260
through GitHub pull requests.

00:33:26.260 --> 00:33:28.690
And so essentially an
upgrade in our world

00:33:28.690 --> 00:33:31.240
is a GitHub pull request,
and then Atlantis

00:33:31.240 --> 00:33:33.650
runs Terraform and makes
all those things happen.

00:33:33.650 --> 00:33:38.200
So it's really elegant
for us, but there's always

00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:41.202
room for improvement
in that sort of thing.

00:33:41.202 --> 00:33:42.660
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
Yeah, for us

00:33:42.660 --> 00:33:44.970
it's all driven by
our operator, which

00:33:44.970 --> 00:33:46.770
is a daemon that we wrote.

00:33:46.770 --> 00:33:50.520
It runs in Kubernetes, but
it isn't part of Kubernetes.

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:52.110
It could run anywhere we put it.

00:33:52.110 --> 00:33:56.040
We just run everything in
Kubernetes, so that too.

00:33:56.040 --> 00:33:58.890
And it goes, it calls
the API for Cloud SQL,

00:33:58.890 --> 00:34:01.170
creates it, waits
for it to show up,

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:02.640
makes sure it does
what it wants,

00:34:02.640 --> 00:34:04.413
and then it creates
everything else.

00:34:04.413 --> 00:34:05.580
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: All right.

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:08.070
So I think we are almost
at the time for Q&amp;A,

00:34:08.070 --> 00:34:09.980
but I want to ask
the last question.

00:34:09.980 --> 00:34:12.445
How many DBAs do you
have in your team?

00:34:12.445 --> 00:34:14.070
JOSEPH SOKOL-MARGOLIS:
Just me, really.

00:34:16.620 --> 00:34:18.659
JOE HUGHES: I would
say zero only because I

00:34:18.659 --> 00:34:20.758
don't consider myself a DBA.

00:34:20.758 --> 00:34:22.050
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: That's great.

00:34:22.050 --> 00:34:23.670
JOE HUGHES: I am the guy
that's on call, I guess.

00:34:23.670 --> 00:34:24.590
AMY KRISHNAMOHAN: OK.

00:34:24.590 --> 00:34:25.090
Awesome.

00:34:25.090 --> 00:34:25.739
Thank you.

00:34:25.739 --> 00:34:29.389
[MUSIC PLAYING]

